camchrome: a macOS or Android persona stores NaNs as an ARM CPU makes them.

IEEE 754 leaves the sign of the NaN an invalid operation makes to the hardware: 0/0 is
0xFFF8000000000000 on x86 and 0x7FF8000000000000 on ARM, and a page reads that bit back through a
typed array or a DataView. The Clearcote audit (cpu-arch-nan-vs-uach) compares it with
userAgentData.architecture, so a macOS persona (Apple Silicon, "arm") on an x86 host failed; an
Android persona on x86 is the emulator tell. With such a persona, a NaN stored into a Float16,
Float32 or Float64 array or through a DataView setter loses its sign bit, as camoufox does for an
ARM identity on x86 (cpu-vendor-estimates.patch).

Where it applies: TypedElementsAccessor::FromScalar (the runtime), CSA StoreElementTypedArray and
the Float16 narrowing (the builtins and ICs), DataViewSet (Torque), Turboshaft StoreTypedElement
and StoreDataViewElement (TurboFan). Maglev leaves float typed-array and DataView stores to the
builtins under such a persona. The flag is read from the command line as 965 reads it, once per
isolate, and the builtins load it through an external reference. mksnapshot warns about, and skips,
the builtins PGO profile of the store builtins this changes instead of aborting.

Not covered: a NaN WebAssembly stores into its own memory.
